F \cos 30^{\circ}=m g \sin 30^{\circ}

$\begin{aligned} & \Rightarrow \quad B I l \cos 30^{\circ}=m g \sin 30^{\circ} \\ & \Rightarrow \quad B I l \times \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}=m g \frac{1}{2} \Rightarrow \sqrt{3} B I=\left(\frac{m}{l}\right) g\end{aligned}$
I=\left(\frac{m}{l}\right) g \cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{3} B}=0.5 \times 10 \times \frac{1}{\sqrt{3} \times 0.25}=11.32 \mathrm{~A}

Given, length of solenoid, $l=50 \mathrm{~cm}=0.5 \mathrm{~m}$
Number of turns, $N=100$
Current, $I=2.5 \mathrm{~A}$

Number of turns per unit length in the solenoid

n=\frac{N}{l}=\frac{100}{0.5}=200 \text { turns } / \mathrm{m}

Magnetic field at the one end of solenoid,

\begin{aligned}
B & =\frac{\mu_0 n I}{2}=\frac{4 \pi \times 10^{-7} \times 200 \times 2.5}{2} \\
& =10 \pi \times 10^{-5}=\pi \times 10^{-4}=3.14 \times 10^{-4} \mathrm{~T}
\end{aligned}

Magnetic field on the axis current carrying circular coil,

B=\frac{\mu_0 I r^2 n}{2\left(x^2+r^2\right)^{3 / 2}}

where, $I=$ current in the coil,
$n=$ number of turns,
$r=$ radius of coil.
and $x=$ distance of the point of observation from centre of coil.
Here, $x=\sqrt{3} r$
\begin{aligned}
\therefore \quad B & =\frac{\mu_0 I r^2 n}{2\left[(\sqrt{3} r)^2+r^2\right]^{3 / 2}}=\frac{\mu_0 I r^2 n}{2\left[3 r^2+r^2\right]^{3 / 2}} \\
& =\frac{\mu_0 I T n}{2\left(4 r^2\right)^{3 / 2}}=\frac{\mu_0 I r^2 n}{2\left[(2 r)^2\right]^{3 / 2}}=\frac{\mu_0 I r^2 n}{2 \times 8 r^3}=\frac{\mu_0 n I}{16 r}
\end{aligned}

(B)<br>\[
\begin{array}{l}
T=2 \pi \sqrt{\frac{I}{M B}} \\
2=2 \pi \sqrt{\frac{8 \times 10^{-6}}{5 \times 10^{-2} \times B}} \\
\text { Squaring } 1=\frac{\Pi^{2} \times 8 \times 10^{-6}}{5 \times 10^{-2} \times B} \\
\therefore B=\frac{3.14^{2} \times 8 \times 10^{-6}}{5}=1.6 \times 10^{-4} \mathrm{~T}
\end{array}
\]

(B)Sol. Magnetic field inside a toroid<br>\[
\mathrm{B}=\mu_{o} \mathrm{nl}
\]<br>Magnetic energy density inside the toroid is<br>\[
\begin{array}{l}
u_{o}=\frac{B^{2}}{2 \mu_{o}}=\frac{\mu_{o}^{2} n^{2} I^{2}}{2 \mu_{o}}=\frac{\mu_{o} n^{2} I^{2}}{2} \\
=\frac{4 \Pi \times 10^{-7} \times(500)^{2} \times 2^{2}}{2}=0.628 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~m}^{-3}
\end{array}
\]

The radius of the circular path followed by an electron in uniform magnetic field is given by<br>\[
\begin{array}{l}
r=\frac{m v}{q B} \\
\Rightarrow r \propto \frac{1}{B}
\end{array}
\]

The radius of circular path is given as<br>\[
\begin{array}{l}
r=\frac{m v \sin \theta}{q} B \\
\Rightarrow r \propto v
\end{array}
\]<br>Thus in a cyclotron, as radius of the circular path of charged particle increases \( v \) increases and \( \omega \) remains unchanged.

Magnetic field at \( O \) due to inner current carrying wire is<br>\[
\begin{array}{l}
B_{1}=\frac{\mu_{0}}{4 \Pi} \frac{2 \Pi(1 / 6) I}{R_{1}}=\frac{\mu_{0}}{12} \frac{I}{R_{1}} \\
B_{2}=\frac{\mu_{0}}{4 \Pi} \frac{2 \Pi(1 / 6) I}{R_{2}}=\frac{\mu_{0}}{12} \frac{I}{R_{2}}
\end{array}
\]<br>Therefore, net magnetic field at \( \mathrm{O} \) is given as<br>\[
B=B_{1}-B_{2}=\frac{\mu_{0}}{12} \frac{I}{R_{1}}-\frac{\mu_{0}}{12} \frac{I}{R_{2}}=\frac{\mu_{0} I}{12}\left(\frac{1}{R_{1}}-\frac{1}{R_{2}}\right)
\]

Consider the two parallel wires as shown in the following figure.<br><img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkcet%252Fsol_133.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkcet%2Fsol_133.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkcet%2Fsol_133.png?alt=media"><br>Now, force experienced by the two wires is<br>\[
F=\frac{\mu_{0}}{4_{\Pi}} \frac{2 I_{1} I_{2}}{d}
\]<br>We know \( I_{1}=1 A ; I_{2}=3 A ; d=1 m \) and \( \frac{\mu_{0}}{4 \Pi}=10^{-7} \)<br>Therefore, \( F=\frac{10^{-7} \times 2 \times 1 \times 3}{1}=6 \times 10^{-7} \)<br>and since the currents are in opposite directions, therefore, force experienced will be repulsive<br>So, the force per unit length acting between two wires is \( 6 \times 10^{-7} \mathrm{~N} \mathrm{~m}^{-1} \) and it is repulsive.

If there is no torsion in the suspension thread, then the time period of a magnet executing SHM is<br>\[
T=2 \pi \sqrt{\frac{I}{M B}}
\]

